  "body": "As far as I understand, the HTML parsing algorithm currently defines the following as a parse error, due to the presence of the unescaped U+003C (less-than sign) within the SVG `script` element.\n\n```\n<svg width='320px' height='320px'>\n  <script type='text/javascript'>\n    if (1 < 2) a = 0;\n  </script>\n</svg>\n```\n\nI believe the reason that’s a parse error is that per the SVG spec, that U+003C is not allowed in any SVG element—similar to the way U+003C is not allowed in most HTML elements (what the HTML spec calls [“normal elements“](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#normal-elements)), but instead only in the HTML [“raw text elements”](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#raw-text-elements) `script` and `style` and the HTML [“escapable raw text element”](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#escapable-raw-text-elements) `textarea` and `title`—and the HTML spec does not classify the SVG `script` element as a “raw text element” covered by special rules in the HTML parsing algorithm\n\nHowever, given the following:\n- the U+003C character in the SVG `script` element goes into the DOM as expected and browsers execute the content of that SVG `script` element as expected; and\n- for the case of an HTML `script` element, the HTML parsing algorithm does not define that U+003C less-than sign as a parse error\n\n…then maybe we should consider updating the HTML parsing algorithm to require the same specific  [“raw text elements”](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#raw-text-elements) handling for SVG `script` elements as it does for HTML `script` elements.\n",
